-with one key per run and a restore prefix restricted to the operating system,
-architecture, Node, Bun and lockfile. Vite Task, Vitest and TypeScript check
-their inputs before reusing the data. After the tests with coverage, the cache
-is uploaded to GitHub, even when an earlier check fails. Vite Task only keeps
-successful results and Vitest keeps transformations, so a failure does not
-become a reused test result. The workflow waits for the upload before ending;
-cache failures neither waive nor fail the checks. A run without cache does the
-same checks. Reusing Vite Task between runners is experimental: check the hits
-in the logs and compare the time saved with the cost of transferring the cache.
-
-In the CI, only `Quality` uploads the package cache, and only when the lockfile
-key does not exist and the job ends successfully. The `setup-project` action
-only restores by default (`cache-save: 'false'`), and `Quality` is the only job
-that passes `cache-save: 'true'`. `Quality`, `Tooling` and the two E2E jobs also
-pass `cache-toolchain: 'true'`: `setup-vp` keeps Vite+, Node and Bun in the
-Actions cache, and only `Quality` uploads that cache. The project trusts the
-cache scope of `main`: on `main` only caches written by `main` itself are
-restored, because GitHub writes the caches of a PR to the merge ref, which
-`main` does not read. That is why `E2E (production)`, which receives the
-`VERCEL_TOKEN` only on the push to `main` and generates the deploy artifact,
-restores packages and toolchain like the other E2E jobs. `Reuse proof`, the
-deploy and the release automation do not pass `cache-toolchain` and prepare the
-toolchain without that cache. `Reuse proof` and the deploy also pass
-`install-dependencies: false`: the action prepares Vite+, Node and Bun from
-`package.json`, without a package cache or `vp install`. The deploy only runs
-`vp dlx` on the tested artifact. With `cache: false`, `setup-vp` neither
-restores nor saves the cache, and `cache-save` and `cache-dependency-path` have
-no effect. The release automation uses the same action: the plan job, without
-write permission, installs the dependencies and restores the package cache of
-`main` without uploading it; the jobs with write permission pass
-`install-dependencies: false` and neither install nor import packages, according
-to the
